window.NOTES = {"1":{"title":"Introduction to Object-Oriented Programming","intro":"OOP organizes a program around objects that hold state and expose behaviour. Instead of thinking only in terms of a sequence of instructions, you model entities and the relationships between them.","topics":[{"title":"1.1 Introduction to OOP","explanation":"Object-oriented programming (OOP) models a system as objects created from classes. A class describes what an object has (fields/state) and what it can do (methods/behaviour). OOP helps large programs become easier to organize, reuse, test and maintain.","code":null,"codeExplanation":null,"example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"1.1.1 Encapsulation","explanation":"Encapsulation means keeping related data and methods together and controlling how the internal data is accessed. A common Java pattern is a private field with public methods that validate or update it. Encapsulation protects an object's internal state rather than allowing arbitrary direct changes.","code":"class BankAccount {\n    private double balance;\n\n    public void deposit(double amount) {\n        if (amount > 0) balance += amount;\n    }\n\n    public double getBalance() {\n        return balance;\n    }\n}","codeExplanation":"private prevents outside code from directly changing balance. deposit() is the controlled operation; getBalance() provides controlled reading.","example":"If code outside the class tries account.balance = -500, it fails because balance is private.","exam":"Exam trap: encapsulation is about controlled access, not simply putting everything inside a class.","compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"1.1.2 Inheritance","explanation":"Inheritance allows a subclass to reuse and specialize members of a superclass. Java uses extends for class inheritance and supports one direct superclass for a class. Use inheritance when there is a genuine is-a relationship.","code":"class Animal {\n    void sound() { System.out.println(\"Animal\"); }\n}\n\nclass Dog extends Animal {\n    @Override\n    void sound() { System.out.println(\"Dog\"); }\n}","codeExplanation":"Dog extends Animal, so Dog is an Animal. @Override marks a method that replaces the inherited implementation.","example":"Dog d = new Dog(); d.sound(); prints Dog.","exam":"Do not confuse inheritance with composition: inheritance models is-a; composition models has-a.","compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"1.1.3 Polymorphism","explanation":"Polymorphism allows one reference type to represent objects of different compatible classes. With overriding, a parent reference can hold a child object and the overridden instance method is selected at runtime (dynamic method dispatch).","code":"Animal a = new Dog();\na.sound();","codeExplanation":"The reference is declared Animal, but the actual object is Dog, so the overridden Dog.sound() runs.","example":"If Cat also extends Animal and overrides sound(), the same Animal variable can refer to a Cat object later.","exam":"The declared reference type affects what members are accessible; the actual object type controls an overridden instance method at runtime.","compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"1.1.4 Abstraction","explanation":"Abstraction focuses attention on essential behaviour while hiding unnecessary implementation detail. Java commonly expresses abstraction through abstract classes and interfaces.","code":"abstract class Shape {\n    abstract double area();\n\n    void describe() {\n        System.out.println(\"A shape\");\n    }\n}\n\nclass Circle extends Shape {\n    private double r;\n    Circle(double r) { this.r = r; }\n    @Override double area() { return Math.PI * r * r; }\n}","codeExplanation":"Shape cannot be instantiated directly. It can provide concrete behaviour and require subclasses to implement abstract behaviour.","example":"Shape s = new Circle(2); s.area() uses Circle's implementation.","exam":"Abstraction is about what an object must do; encapsulation is about controlling access to how its state is stored.","compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"1.2 Interface","explanation":"An interface defines a contract that implementing classes agree to provide. A class uses implements and can implement multiple interfaces. Interfaces are useful when unrelated classes need to share a common capability.","code":"interface Flyable {\n    void fly();\n\n    default void land() {\n        System.out.println(\"Landing\");\n    }\n}\n\nclass Bird implements Flyable {\n    public void fly() { System.out.println(\"Flying\"); }\n}","codeExplanation":"Bird must implement fly(). The default land() method already has an implementation, so Bird may inherit it.","example":"Flyable f = new Bird(); f.fly(); prints Flying.","exam":"A class can extend one class but implement multiple interfaces.","compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"Overloading vs overriding","explanation":"Overloading uses the same method name with different parameter lists in the same class/inheritance context; selection is made at compile time. Overriding occurs when a subclass supplies a compatible implementation of an inherited instance method; selection occurs at runtime for an object call.","code":"class Calc {\n    int add(int a, int b) { return a+b; }\n    int add(int a, int b, int c) { return a+b+c; }\n}","codeExplanation":"The two add methods are overloaded because their parameter lists differ.","example":"add(2,3) selects the two-parameter method; add(2,3,4) selects the three-parameter method.","exam":"Return type alone cannot distinguish overloaded methods.","compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"Core OOP relationships","explanation":"An is-a relationship is usually represented by inheritance. A has-a relationship is commonly represented by composition or aggregation. Prefer the simplest relationship that accurately models the problem.","code":"class Engine {}\nclass Car {\n    private Engine engine = new Engine();\n}","codeExplanation":"Car has an Engine, so composition models the relationship without making Engine a subclass of Car.","example":"Car is-a Vehicle would be an inheritance relationship.","exam":"Overusing inheritance can make classes tightly coupled and hard to change.","compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"Exam recognition","explanation":"When you see private fields plus public getters/setters, think encapsulation. When you see extends, think inheritance. When you see a parent reference holding a child object, think polymorphism. When you see abstract or interface, think abstraction/contract.","code":null,"codeExplanation":null,"example":null,"exam":"These recognition clues are frequently useful in code-output questions.","compare":null,"complexity":null}]},"2":{"title":"Class Hierarchies, Packages, Namespaces & Modularity","intro":"Class hierarchies organize inheritance relationships. Good hierarchy design balances reuse with loose coupling, while packages, namespaces, documentation, testing and version control keep larger Java projects manageable.","topics":[{"title":"2.1 Introduction","explanation":"A class hierarchy arranges classes according to inheritance relationships. A superclass provides common members and subclasses specialize them. Java's class hierarchy ultimately has java.lang.Object at the root.","code":null,"codeExplanation":null,"example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"2.2 Fundamentals of Class Hierarchies","explanation":"A hierarchy should reflect meaningful relationships. A subclass should be substitutable for its superclass where the model says it is an is-a relationship.","code":"class Animal { void move() { System.out.println(\"move\"); } }\nclass Dog extends Animal { void bark() { System.out.println(\"bark\"); } }","codeExplanation":"Dog inherits move() and adds bark().","example":"Dog d = new Dog(); d.move(); d.bark();","exam":"One direct superclass per Java class; interfaces can provide additional contracts.","compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"2.2.1 Single and Multiple Inheritance","explanation":"Java classes support single inheritance: one direct superclass. Java does not allow a class to extend two classes. Multiple inheritance of type can be achieved through multiple interfaces.","code":"interface A { void a(); }\ninterface B { void b(); }\nclass C implements A, B {\n    public void a() {}\n    public void b() {}\n}","codeExplanation":"C implements two interfaces, so it satisfies both contracts without extending two classes.","example":"class C extends A, B is illegal Java syntax if A and B are classes.","exam":"Exam trap: 'multiple inheritance' through interfaces is not the same as extending multiple classes.","compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"2.2.2 Interface Usage","explanation":"Interfaces let a class promise specific behaviour without forcing it into a single class family. They are useful for capabilities, loose coupling and polymorphic APIs.","code":"interface Payable { double pay(); }\nclass Employee implements Payable {\n    public double pay() { return 50000; }\n}","codeExplanation":"Employee agrees to the Payable contract by implementing pay().","example":"Payable p = new Employee(); System.out.println(p.pay());","exam":"Use implements for interfaces; use extends for a class-to-class inheritance relationship.","compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"2.3 Benefits of Class Hierarchies","explanation":"Major benefits include code reuse, improved maintenance, clearer organization, polymorphism and easier extension when the hierarchy is well designed.","code":null,"codeExplanation":null,"example":null,"exam":"A benefit becomes a drawback when the hierarchy is forced or excessively deep.","compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"2.3.1 Code Reusability","explanation":"Common fields and methods can be defined once in a superclass and reused by subclasses. This reduces duplication, but inherited behaviour must still make sense for every subclass.","code":null,"codeExplanation":null,"example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"2.3.2 Improved Maintenance","explanation":"A shared implementation can be corrected or improved in one place and benefit multiple subclasses. However, changes to a superclass can affect many descendants, so coupling must be considered.","code":null,"codeExplanation":null,"example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"2.3.3 Enhanced Readability and Organization","explanation":"A meaningful hierarchy communicates domain relationships. A reader can understand that Dog is an Animal without reading every implementation detail.","code":null,"codeExplanation":null,"example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"2.4 Designing Effective Class Hierarchies","explanation":"Start with real domain relationships, keep abstractions meaningful, avoid unnecessary depth, and use polymorphism to reduce conditional logic.","code":null,"codeExplanation":null,"example":null,"exam":"A good hierarchy should make the program easier to understand, not merely increase the number of classes.","compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"2.4.1 Abstraction","explanation":"Place common concepts at an appropriate abstraction level. Abstract classes can hold shared state/implementation while requiring subclasses to provide specific behaviour.","code":null,"codeExplanation":null,"example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"2.4.2 Encapsulation","explanation":"Keep implementation details protected. A hierarchy should not force subclasses or external callers to depend on internal representation unnecessarily.","code":null,"codeExplanation":null,"example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"2.4.3 Polymorphism","explanation":"Program to a superclass or interface when appropriate. This lets client code work with different implementations through a common type.","code":"Animal a = new Dog();\na.sound();","codeExplanation":"The common Animal reference lets client code call the shared contract while the runtime object supplies the overridden behaviour.","example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"2.5 Common Pitfalls","explanation":"Common problems include deep inheritance chains, tight coupling and overusing inheritance where composition would be clearer.","code":null,"codeExplanation":null,"example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"2.5.1 Deep Inheritance Chains","explanation":"Very deep chains make behaviour harder to trace and changes harder to predict. Prefer a shallow, meaningful hierarchy.","code":null,"codeExplanation":null,"example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"2.5.2 Tight Coupling","explanation":"Subclasses can become strongly dependent on superclass implementation details. A change in the parent can ripple through descendants.","code":null,"codeExplanation":null,"example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"2.5.3 Overusing Inheritance","explanation":"Do not inherit simply to reuse a few methods. Composition often gives more flexibility when the relationship is has-a rather than is-a.","code":null,"codeExplanation":null,"example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"2.6 Packages, Namespaces and Modularity","explanation":"Packages group related types, reduce naming collisions and support access control. Java packages act as the primary namespace mechanism.","code":"package com.example.school;\n\npublic class Student {\n    // ...\n}","codeExplanation":"The package declaration associates Student with com.example.school. The directory structure normally mirrors the package name.","example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"2.6.1 Packages","explanation":"Use package declarations and imports to organize types. Fully qualified names identify a type precisely when needed.","code":"import java.util.ArrayList;\nArrayList<String> names = new ArrayList<>();","codeExplanation":"import lets the code use ArrayList without repeatedly writing java.util.ArrayList.","example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"2.6.2 Namespaces","explanation":"A namespace provides a naming context so similarly named types can coexist. Java packages serve this purpose.","code":null,"codeExplanation":null,"example":"com.school.Student and com.company.Student can both have the simple name Student because their package-qualified names differ.","exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"2.6.3 Modularization","explanation":"Modularization divides a system into coherent components with clear responsibilities and boundaries. It reduces complexity and makes testing and maintenance easier.","code":null,"codeExplanation":null,"example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"2.6.4 Documentation","explanation":"Documentation explains purpose, assumptions, APIs and important decisions. Clear documentation helps future maintainers understand code without reverse-engineering it.","code":null,"codeExplanation":null,"example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"2.6.5 Code Reviews and Standards","explanation":"Code reviews catch defects and improve consistency. Coding standards make naming, formatting and structure predictable across a project.","code":null,"codeExplanation":null,"example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"2.6.6 Version Control","explanation":"Version control records changes, supports collaboration and lets developers inspect or restore earlier versions. Git is a common example.","code":null,"codeExplanation":null,"example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"2.6.7 Testing Practices","explanation":"Tests check whether code behaves as expected. Unit tests focus on small components; integration tests examine interactions between components.","code":null,"codeExplanation":null,"example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"2.6.8 Continuous Integration / CI-CD","explanation":"Continuous integration automatically builds and tests changes frequently. Delivery/deployment pipelines automate later stages of releasing software.","code":null,"codeExplanation":null,"example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"2.6.9 Deployment","explanation":"Deployment moves a tested application into an environment where it can be used. Good deployment processes are repeatable and traceable.","code":null,"codeExplanation":null,"example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"2.7 Design Patterns","explanation":"A design pattern is a reusable solution idea for a recurring design problem. It is a template, not a copy-and-paste program.","code":null,"codeExplanation":null,"example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"Java Object class and standard APIs","explanation":"Every class ultimately inherits from Object. Common methods include toString(), equals(Object) and hashCode(). Java's standard APIs provide reusable classes and interfaces in packages such as java.lang and java.util.","code":"class Student {\n    String name;\n    public String toString() { return name; }\n}\nStudent s = new Student();\ns.name = \"Ada\";\nSystem.out.println(s);","codeExplanation":"Printing s invokes toString() through String conversion of the object reference.","example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null}]},"3":{"title":"Iterators, Lists, Stacks, Queues & Java Collections Framework","intro":"Java Collections provide reusable data structures and traversal mechanisms. The exam focus is often method behaviour, ordering, duplicates, iterator movement and the differences between similar APIs.","topics":[{"title":"3.1 Iterators and Enumerators in Java","explanation":"Traversal separates the process of visiting elements from the collection's internal representation.","code":null,"codeExplanation":null,"example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"3.1.1 Iterator","explanation":"Iterator uses hasNext(), next() and, when supported, remove(). hasNext() checks whether another element exists; next() advances and returns it.","code":"List<Integer> nums = new ArrayList<>();\nnums.add(10); nums.add(20);\nIterator<Integer> it = nums.iterator();\nwhile (it.hasNext()) {\n    System.out.println(it.next());\n}","codeExplanation":"First hasNext() is true, next() returns 10 and advances; the second returns 20; then hasNext() becomes false.","example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"3.1.2 Enumeration","explanation":"Enumeration is an older traversal API commonly associated with legacy collections. It uses hasMoreElements() and nextElement() and does not provide Iterator.remove().","code":"Enumeration<Integer> e = vector.elements();\nwhile (e.hasMoreElements()) {\n    System.out.println(e.nextElement());\n}","codeExplanation":"hasMoreElements() checks for another element and nextElement() returns it.","example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"3.2 Lists, Stacks and Queues","explanation":"List is an ordered collection; Stack models LIFO; Queue normally models FIFO. The correct method depends on the collection and the desired empty-state behaviour.","code":null,"codeExplanation":null,"example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"3.2.1 List","explanation":"List preserves order, supports positional access and normally allows duplicates. ArrayList is array-backed; LinkedList uses linked nodes.","code":"List<String> names = new ArrayList<>();\nnames.add(\"A\");\nnames.add(\"B\");\nnames.add(\"A\");\nSystem.out.println(names.get(1));","codeExplanation":"get(1) returns B because list indexes start at zero; duplicates are allowed.","example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"3.2.2 Stack","explanation":"Stack is LIFO: the last item pushed is the first item popped. Important methods are push(), pop(), peek(), empty()/isEmpty() and search().","code":"Stack<Integer> s = new Stack<>();\ns.push(10);\ns.push(20);\nSystem.out.println(s.peek());\nSystem.out.println(s.pop());","codeExplanation":"peek() prints 20 without removing it; pop() then removes and returns 20.","example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"3.2.3 Queue","explanation":"A queue is normally FIFO. offer()/add() insert, poll()/remove() remove the head, and peek()/element() inspect the head. poll and peek return null when empty; remove and element throw when empty.","code":"Queue<Integer> q = new LinkedList<>();\nq.offer(10);\nq.offer(20);\nSystem.out.println(q.peek());\nSystem.out.println(q.poll());","codeExplanation":"peek() returns 10 without removing it; poll() returns and removes 10.","example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"3.3 Overview of Java Collections Framework","explanation":"The Collections Framework provides interfaces, implementations and algorithms that work together to represent and manipulate groups of objects.","code":null,"codeExplanation":null,"example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"3.3.1 Introduction","explanation":"Key interfaces include Collection, List, Set, Queue, Deque and Map. Map is separate from Collection because it represents key-value mappings.","code":null,"codeExplanation":null,"example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"3.3.2 Key Interfaces","explanation":"List is ordered and indexed; Set emphasizes uniqueness; Queue represents processing order; Map associates keys with values.","code":null,"codeExplanation":null,"example":null,"exam":"Map is not a subtype of Collection.","compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"3.3.3 Common Collection Classes","explanation":"ArrayList, LinkedList, HashSet, TreeSet, HashMap, PriorityQueue and Stack are common classes covered in this course.","code":null,"codeExplanation":null,"example":null,"exam":"Know the defining behaviour of each class, not only its name.","compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"3.3.4 Algorithms","explanation":"Collections provides utility algorithms such as sort, reverse, shuffle, binarySearch and others. These operate on compatible collections/lists.","code":"List<Integer> a = new ArrayList<>(Arrays.asList(3,1,2));\nCollections.sort(a);\nSystem.out.println(a);","codeExplanation":"Collections.sort orders the list according to its natural ordering or a supplied comparator.","example":"The output is [1, 2, 3].","exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"3.3.5 Advantages","explanation":"Collections reduce the need to build data structures from scratch, improve reuse, provide tested implementations and make APIs more consistent.","code":null,"codeExplanation":null,"example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"3.3.6 ArrayList Example","explanation":"ArrayList is a resizable array. It is usually strong for indexed reads and appending, while inserting/removing near the middle can require shifting elements.","code":"ArrayList<String> a = new ArrayList<>();\na.add(\"A\"); a.add(\"B\");\na.add(1, \"X\");\nSystem.out.println(a);","codeExplanation":"The insertion at index 1 shifts B to the right, producing [A, X, B].","example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"3.3.7 HashMap Example","explanation":"HashMap stores key-value mappings. Keys are unique; putting a value for an existing key replaces the previous value.","code":"HashMap<String,Integer> m = new HashMap<>();\nm.put(\"A\", 10);\nm.put(\"A\", 20);\nSystem.out.println(m.get(\"A\"));","codeExplanation":"The second put replaces the value for key A, so get returns 20.","example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"3.3.8 HashSet Example","explanation":"HashSet stores unique elements and does not promise sorted order. Adding an element that is already present does not create a duplicate.","code":"HashSet<Integer> s = new HashSet<>();\ns.add(5); s.add(5);\nSystem.out.println(s.size());","codeExplanation":"The size is 1 because the duplicate value is not stored twice.","example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"3.3.9 LinkedList Example","explanation":"LinkedList implements both List and Deque/Queue operations. It is useful when linked-node insertion/removal behaviour is appropriate.","code":"LinkedList<Integer> l = new LinkedList<>();\nl.add(10); l.add(20);\nl.addFirst(5);\nSystem.out.println(l);","codeExplanation":"addFirst places 5 at the front, giving [5, 10, 20].","example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"3.3.10 TreeSet Example","explanation":"TreeSet stores unique elements in sorted order according to natural ordering or a comparator.","code":"TreeSet<Integer> t = new TreeSet<>();\nt.add(30); t.add(10); t.add(20);\nSystem.out.println(t);","codeExplanation":"The set prints in ascending order: [10, 20, 30].","example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"3.3.11 PriorityQueue Example","explanation":"PriorityQueue removes according to priority, not simple insertion order. With natural ordering for integers, the smallest element is at the head.","code":"PriorityQueue<Integer> p = new PriorityQueue<>();\np.offer(30); p.offer(10); p.offer(20);\nSystem.out.println(p.poll());","codeExplanation":"poll() returns 10 under natural integer ordering.","example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"3.3.12 Stack Example","explanation":"Stack uses LIFO operations. Modern Java often prefers Deque/ArrayDeque for stack behaviour, but Stack remains important for this course.","code":"Stack<String> s = new Stack<>();\ns.push(\"A\"); s.push(\"B\");\nSystem.out.println(s.pop());","codeExplanation":"B is removed first because it was pushed last.","example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"3.3.13 Queue Example","explanation":"LinkedList can be used as a Queue. The head is the next element to be processed.","code":"Queue<String> q = new LinkedList<>();\nq.add(\"A\"); q.add(\"B\");\nSystem.out.println(q.remove());","codeExplanation":"remove() returns A because the queue is FIFO.","example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"3.3.14 Fail-Safe Iterator terminology","explanation":"Fail-fast iterators commonly detect structural modification and throw ConcurrentModificationException. 'Fail-safe' is a common teaching term for traversal that works over a snapshot/copy or a weakly consistent mechanism; exact behaviour depends on the collection.","code":"List<Integer> a = new ArrayList<>(Arrays.asList(1,2,3));\nfor (Integer x : a) {\n    if (x == 2) a.remove(x);\n}","codeExplanation":"Direct structural modification during enhanced-for iteration can cause ConcurrentModificationException. Use Iterator.remove() when supported.","example":"The safe pattern is Iterator<Integer> it = a.iterator(); while(it.hasNext()){ if(...) it.remove(); else it.next(); }","exam":"Exam trap: do not treat 'fail-safe' as a formal interface shared by all Java collections.","compare":null,"complexity":null}]},"4":{"title":"Iterator Pattern in Depth","intro":"The Iterator design pattern provides a standard way to traverse a collection without exposing its internal representation. Java's fail-fast/fail-safe behaviour is a major exam area.","topics":[{"title":"4.1 Iterator Pattern in Depth","explanation":"A design pattern captures a reusable design idea. The Iterator pattern separates traversal from the aggregate being traversed.","code":null,"codeExplanation":null,"example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"4.1.1 Introduction to Design Patterns","explanation":"Patterns describe recurring design solutions, their participants and trade-offs. They are not libraries or complete applications.","code":null,"codeExplanation":null,"example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"4.1.2 Design Patterns","explanation":"A pattern normally identifies a context/problem, a general solution structure and consequences. Good use of patterns improves consistency but unnecessary patterns add complexity.","code":null,"codeExplanation":null,"example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"4.1.3 Benefits of Iterator Pattern","explanation":"Clients can traverse different collections through a common interface while hiding the collection's internal representation. Traversal logic becomes more reusable.","code":null,"codeExplanation":null,"example":null,"exam":"The iterator exposes traversal operations, not the collection's internal storage structure.","compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"4.1.4 Implementation of Iterator Pattern","explanation":"A custom iterator usually stores traversal state and implements hasNext() and next(). A collection provides an iterator() method that creates the iterator.","code":"class CountIterator implements Iterator<Integer> {\n    private int current = 1;\n    public boolean hasNext() { return current <= 3; }\n    public Integer next() { return current++; }\n}","codeExplanation":"hasNext() answers whether traversal can continue; next() returns the current value and advances state.","example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"4.1.5 Custom Iterator","explanation":"A custom iterator can define exactly how a structure is visited. The key is that client code should not need to know the structure's representation.","code":"Iterator<Integer> it = new CountIterator();\nwhile (it.hasNext()) {\n    System.out.println(it.next());\n}","codeExplanation":"The loop prints 1, 2, 3 because current advances after each next().","example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"4.1.6 Fail-Fast Iterator","explanation":"A fail-fast iterator commonly detects structural modification outside the iterator and throws ConcurrentModificationException on a later traversal operation.","code":"List<String> names = new ArrayList<>(Arrays.asList(\"A\",\"B\"));\nfor (String n : names) {\n    names.add(\"C\");\n}","codeExplanation":"The enhanced-for loop uses an iterator; modifying the list structurally outside that iterator can trigger ConcurrentModificationException.","example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"4.1.7 Fail-Safe Iterator","explanation":"The term is used in many teaching materials for iterators that traverse a snapshot/copy or use weakly consistent mechanisms. Such traversal may continue without the same fail-fast exception, depending on the collection.","code":null,"codeExplanation":null,"example":null,"exam":"Always answer based on the collection's documented behaviour rather than assuming every iterator is 'fail-safe'.","compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"4.1.8 Fail-Fast vs Fail-Safe","explanation":"Fail-fast prioritizes early detection of unexpected structural modification; snapshot/weakly consistent traversal prioritizes continuing traversal under defined concurrent-use semantics. The exact implementation matters.","code":null,"codeExplanation":null,"example":null,"exam":"Do not claim that fail-safe means 'never throws any exception'.","compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"Safe modification during iteration","explanation":"When the iterator supports removal, Iterator.remove() removes the last element returned by next() without directly modifying the collection through another reference.","code":"Iterator<String> it = names.iterator();\nwhile (it.hasNext()) {\n    String n = it.next();\n    if (n.equals(\"A\")) it.remove();\n}","codeExplanation":"remove() is tied to the last next() call. Calling remove twice without another next() is illegal for the iterator.","example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null}]},"5":{"title":"Searching Algorithms","intro":"Searching means locating a target value. Linear search examines candidates one by one; binary search repeatedly halves an ordered search range.","topics":[{"title":"5.1 Searching Algorithms","explanation":"A search algorithm receives a target and a collection/range and tries to determine whether the target is present and, depending on the algorithm, where it occurs.","code":null,"codeExplanation":null,"example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"5.1.1 Linear Search","explanation":"Linear search checks elements sequentially from one end until the target is found or all elements have been examined. It does not require sorted data.","code":"int[] a = {4, 9, 2, 7, 5};\nint target = 7;\nint index = -1;\nfor (int i = 0; i < a.length; i++) {\n    if (a[i] == target) { index = i; break; }\n}\nSystem.out.println(index);","codeExplanation":"i moves from 0 upward. When a[i] equals 7, index becomes 3 and break stops the loop.","example":"The output is 3.","exam":"Worst-case time: O(n). Best case: O(1) when the first element matches.","compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"5.1.2 Binary Search","explanation":"Binary search requires an ordered search range. It compares the target with the middle element and discards half of the remaining candidates each step.","code":"int[] a = {2, 5, 8, 12, 17};\nint target = 12;\nint low = 0, high = a.length - 1;\nwhile (low <= high) {\n    int mid = (low + high) / 2;\n    if (a[mid] == target) {\n        System.out.println(mid);\n        break;\n    } else if (target < a[mid]) high = mid - 1;\n    else low = mid + 1;\n}","codeExplanation":"The middle value is checked. If target is smaller, high moves left; if target is larger, low moves right.","example":"The target 12 is found at index 3.","exam":"Worst-case time: O(log n). Iterative extra space: O(1). Recursive stack: O(log n).","compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"5.1.3 Advantages and Disadvantages","explanation":"Linear search is simple and works on unsorted data but can be slow. Binary search is much faster on large ordered data but requires sorted data and correct boundary updates.","code":null,"codeExplanation":null,"example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"5.1.4 Searching in Arrays","explanation":"Java arrays use zero-based indexing. For repeated searching, you may choose a data structure or algorithm suited to whether the data remains ordered.","code":"int[] numbers = {3, 6, 9, 12};\nint pos = Arrays.binarySearch(numbers, 9);\nSystem.out.println(pos);","codeExplanation":"Arrays.binarySearch returns the index of a found element when the array is sorted according to the search method's ordering.","example":"The output is 2.","exam":"A common exam trap is confusing the returned index with the target value.","compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"5.1.5 Calculating Time Complexity","explanation":"Count how the number of operations grows as n grows. A single full pass is typically O(n); repeated halving gives O(log n).","code":null,"codeExplanation":null,"example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"5.1.6 Early Exit Strategy","explanation":"If a target has been found, break can prevent unnecessary remaining comparisons. This changes the actual work for that input but does not change linear search's worst-case O(n).","code":"for (int x : a) {\n    if (x == target) {\n        System.out.println(\"Found\");\n        break;\n    }\n}","codeExplanation":"break exits the nearest loop immediately after the target is found.","example":"If target is the first element, only one comparison is needed.","exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null}]},"6":{"title":"Sorting Algorithms & Complexity","intro":"Sorting arranges data according to an order. Learn each algorithm by its signature operation, tracing pattern, time/space complexity, stability and trade-offs.","topics":[{"title":"6.1 Sorting Algorithms","explanation":"Sorting can be ascending, descending, stable or unstable, and in-place or requiring additional storage. The algorithm choice depends on data size and characteristics.","code":null,"codeExplanation":null,"example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"6.1.1 Types of Sorting Algorithms","explanation":"Common algorithms include Bubble, Selection, Insertion, Merge, Quick and Heap Sort. Their mechanics and complexity differ.","code":null,"codeExplanation":null,"example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"6.1.2 Bubble Sort","explanation":"Bubble sort repeatedly compares adjacent elements and swaps them when out of order. After each pass, a largest remaining value can move toward the end.","code":"int[] a = {5, 2, 4};\nfor (int i = 0; i < a.length - 1; i++) {\n    for (int j = 0; j < a.length - 1 - i; j++) {\n        if (a[j] > a[j+1]) {\n            int t = a[j]; a[j] = a[j+1]; a[j+1] = t;\n        }\n    }\n}","codeExplanation":"The key clue is comparing a[j] with a[j+1]. Adjacent out-of-order values are swapped.","example":"The final array is [2, 4, 5].","exam":"Typical/worst O(n²); optimized best O(n) when no swaps occur. Stable in the usual adjacent-swap form.","compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"6.1.3 Selection Sort","explanation":"Selection sort finds the minimum (for ascending order) in the unsorted portion and swaps it into the next final position.","code":"for (int i = 0; i < a.length - 1; i++) {\n    int min = i;\n    for (int j = i + 1; j < a.length; j++) {\n        if (a[j] < a[min]) min = j;\n    }\n    int t = a[i]; a[i] = a[min]; a[min] = t;\n}","codeExplanation":"The clue is the min index. The inner loop searches for the smallest remaining value, then one swap places it.","example":null,"exam":"O(n²) time; O(1) extra space; standard selection sort is not stable.","compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"6.1.4 Insertion Sort","explanation":"Insertion sort grows a sorted prefix. It stores the current value as key, shifts larger values right and inserts key into the gap.","code":"for (int i = 1; i < a.length; i++) {\n    int key = a[i];\n    int j = i - 1;\n    while (j >= 0 && a[j] > key) {\n        a[j+1] = a[j];\n        j--;\n    }\n    a[j+1] = key;\n}","codeExplanation":"The key is temporarily removed from its position; larger sorted-prefix values shift right; key is inserted at j+1.","example":null,"exam":"Worst/typical O(n²), best O(n) for nearly sorted data; O(1) extra; stable.","compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"6.1.5 Merge Sort","explanation":"Merge sort divides the array into halves, recursively sorts the halves, then merges two sorted halves. The important operation is merging, not searching.","code":"void mergeSort(int[] a, int left, int right) {\n    if (left >= right) return;\n    int mid = (left + right) / 2;\n    mergeSort(a, left, mid);\n    mergeSort(a, mid + 1, right);\n    merge(a, left, mid, right);\n}","codeExplanation":"The base case is one element. The array is divided until small pieces remain, then merge() combines sorted halves.","example":null,"exam":"O(n log n) time; standard array implementation uses O(n) auxiliary space; stable when merge chooses equal items consistently.","compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"6.1.6 Quick Sort","explanation":"Quick sort chooses a pivot, partitions the range around that pivot, then recursively sorts the partitions. Performance depends strongly on pivot choices.","code":"void quickSort(int[] a, int low, int high) {\n    if (low < high) {\n        int p = partition(a, low, high);\n        quickSort(a, low, p-1);\n        quickSort(a, p+1, high);\n    }\n}","codeExplanation":"partition places the pivot in a final relative position and returns its index; the recursive calls process the two sides.","example":null,"exam":"Average O(n log n), worst O(n²); recursion stack depends on partition balance; standard quicksort is not stable.","compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"6.1.7 Heap Sort","explanation":"Heap sort builds a heap and repeatedly moves the root into the sorted region, restoring the heap after each removal. For ascending order, a max heap is commonly used.","code":"void heapify(int[] a, int n, int i) {\n    int largest = i;\n    int left = 2*i + 1;\n    int right = 2*i + 2;\n    // compare children and restore max-heap property\n}","codeExplanation":"For a zero-based array, left child index is 2*i+1 and right child is 2*i+2. heapify restores heap order.","example":null,"exam":"O(n log n) time in best/average/worst cases; standard in-place version uses O(1) auxiliary space; not stable.","compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"6.2 Complexity and Big-O Notation","explanation":"Time complexity describes how running work grows with input size; space complexity describes additional memory growth. Big-O is used to describe asymptotic growth/upper-bound behaviour.","code":null,"codeExplanation":null,"example":null,"exam":"For large n, O(n log n) grows much more slowly than O(n²).","compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"6.2.1 What is Big-O Notation?","explanation":"Big-O ignores constant factors and lower-order terms when describing asymptotic growth. For example, 3n² + 5n + 2 is O(n²).","code":null,"codeExplanation":null,"example":"Two nested loops that each run proportional to n commonly give O(n²).","exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"6.2.2 Other Complexity Notations","explanation":"Ω describes an asymptotic lower bound and Θ describes a tight asymptotic bound. In exam questions, distinguish the notation from the algorithm's actual best/worst case being discussed.","code":null,"codeExplanation":null,"example":null,"exam":"Do not automatically call every best case Ω(n); the function and context determine the bound.","compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"Stability and in-place sorting","explanation":"A stable sort preserves the relative order of equal-key records. In-place means the algorithm uses only a small amount of extra memory beyond the input (implementation dependent).","code":null,"codeExplanation":null,"example":null,"exam":"Bubble and insertion are stable in common implementations; selection, quick and heap are not generally stable; merge is commonly stable but uses extra memory.","compare":null,"complexity":null}]},"7":{"title":"Recursive Algorithms","intro":"Recursion solves a problem by making a smaller version of the same problem. Every recursive algorithm needs a base case and a recursive case, and every call consumes stack space.","topics":[{"title":"7.1 Introduction","explanation":"Recursion occurs when a method calls itself directly or indirectly. The recursive call must move toward a base case or the method will not terminate.","code":"static int countdown(int n) {\n    if (n == 0) return 0;\n    System.out.println(n);\n    return countdown(n - 1);\n}","codeExplanation":"The base case stops recursion at n==0. Each call decreases n, so the base case is eventually reached.","example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"7.1.1 Examples of Recursive Algorithms","explanation":"Common examples include factorial, Fibonacci, GCD, string reversal, binary search, quicksort, exponentiation, Tower of Hanoi and permutation generation.","code":null,"codeExplanation":null,"example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"7.1.2 Important Considerations","explanation":"A recursive solution needs a correct base case, progress toward the base case, manageable call depth and acceptable time/space cost.","code":null,"codeExplanation":null,"example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"7.2 Fundamentals of Recursion in Java","explanation":"A recursive Java method has a base case and recursive case. Each call gets its own parameters/local variables and a stack frame.","code":"static int factorial(int n) {\n    if (n <= 1) return 1;\n    return n * factorial(n - 1);\n}","codeExplanation":"factorial(4) becomes 4*factorial(3), then 3*factorial(2), then 2*factorial(1); the base case returns 1 and calls unwind to produce 24.","example":"factorial(4) returns 24.","exam":"Missing/incorrect base cases are a common cause of StackOverflowError.","compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"7.3 Recursive Problem-Solving Techniques","explanation":"Break the problem into a smaller instance, solve the smaller instance recursively, then combine the result. Divide-and-conquer algorithms use this idea extensively.","code":null,"codeExplanation":null,"example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"7.3.1 Tail Recursion and Optimization","explanation":"A recursive call is tail-recursive when it is the final operation of the method. Java does not guarantee automatic tail-call optimization, so deep tail recursion can still consume stack space.","code":"static int sum(int n, int acc) {\n    if (n == 0) return acc;\n    return sum(n - 1, acc + n);\n}","codeExplanation":"The recursive call is the final expression. Despite that form, standard Java does not promise elimination of the stack frame.","example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"7.4.1 Greatest Common Divisor (GCD)","explanation":"Euclid's algorithm repeatedly replaces (a,b) with (b, a%b) until b becomes zero. The remaining a is the GCD.","code":"static int gcd(int a, int b) {\n    if (b == 0) return a;\n    return gcd(b, a % b);\n}","codeExplanation":"For gcd(48,18): gcd(18,12) → gcd(12,6) → gcd(6,0) → 6.","example":"gcd(48,18) returns 6.","exam":"Time is O(log min(a,b)) for Euclid's algorithm under the standard analysis.","compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"7.4.2 Reversing a String Recursively","explanation":"A string can be reversed by taking the first character and placing it after the recursively reversed remainder, or by using indices.","code":"static String reverse(String s) {\n    if (s.length() <= 1) return s;\n    return reverse(s.substring(1)) + s.charAt(0);\n}","codeExplanation":"reverse(\"abc\") becomes reverse(\"bc\") + a, then reverse(\"c\") + b + a, producing cba. Repeated substring concatenation may cost extra memory/time.","example":"reverse(\"abc\") returns cba.","exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"7.4.3 QuickSort Algorithm","explanation":"Quicksort is recursive after partitioning around a pivot. Each recursive call works on a smaller range.","code":"quickSort(a, low, p - 1);\nquickSort(a, p + 1, high);","codeExplanation":"The pivot index p separates the two recursive ranges; p itself is not processed again.","example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"7.4.4 Tower of Hanoi","explanation":"Move n disks from source to destination using an auxiliary peg. Move n-1 disks away, move the largest disk, then move n-1 disks onto it.","code":"static void hanoi(int n, char from, char aux, char to) {\n    if (n == 0) return;\n    hanoi(n-1, from, to, aux);\n    System.out.println(from + \" -> \" + to);\n    hanoi(n-1, aux, from, to);\n}","codeExplanation":"The base case is n==0. The recurrence is T(n)=2T(n-1)+1, giving 2^n-1 moves.","example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"7.4.5 Recursively Computing Exponentiation","explanation":"The straightforward method computes a^(n-1) repeatedly. A faster divide-and-conquer version uses exponentiation by squaring.","code":"static long power(long a, int n) {\n    if (n == 0) return 1;\n    long half = power(a, n/2);\n    if (n % 2 == 0) return half * half;\n    return a * half * half;\n}","codeExplanation":"The exponent is halved each call, so the number of recursive levels is logarithmic.","example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"7.4.6 Generating Permutations","explanation":"Permutation generation recursively fixes one position and recursively permutes the remaining positions, often using swapping/backtracking.","code":"void permute(char[] a, int index) {\n    if (index == a.length) {\n        System.out.println(new String(a));\n        return;\n    }\n    for (int i = index; i < a.length; i++) {\n        char t=a[index]; a[index]=a[i]; a[i]=t;\n        permute(a, index+1);\n        t=a[index]; a[index]=a[i]; a[i]=t;\n    }\n}","codeExplanation":"The swap chooses which character occupies the current position; after recursion, the swap is undone (backtracking).","example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"7.5.1 Stack Memory Management and Overflow","explanation":"Each active recursive call consumes stack memory. Excessive depth can cause StackOverflowError. Iterative solutions may avoid this call-stack cost.","code":null,"codeExplanation":null,"example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"7.5.2 Call Overhead","explanation":"Each recursive call has overhead for creating and managing a stack frame. A mathematically elegant recursive solution can still be slower than a simple loop.","code":null,"codeExplanation":null,"example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"7.5.3 Tail Recursion and Lack of Automatic TCO","explanation":"Tail-recursive form does not guarantee constant stack usage in Java. Do not assume the JVM must transform it into a loop.","code":null,"codeExplanation":null,"example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"7.5.4 Recursive Computations and Memoization","explanation":"Memoization caches results of repeated subproblems. It can turn an exponential naive recursion, such as Fibonacci, into a much more efficient dynamic-programming style solution.","code":"static long fib(int n, long[] memo) {\n    if (n <= 1) return n;\n    if (memo[n] != 0) return memo[n];\n    memo[n] = fib(n-1,memo) + fib(n-2,memo);\n    return memo[n];\n}","codeExplanation":"The memo array prevents recomputing the same Fibonacci values repeatedly.","example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"7.5.5 Compiler/JIT Optimizations","explanation":"The JVM may optimize some code at runtime, but Java does not provide a language-level guarantee of tail-call elimination. Correctness and stack depth should therefore be considered explicitly.","code":null,"codeExplanation":null,"example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"7.6 Recursive vs Iterative Approaches","explanation":"Iteration often uses loops and constant call-stack space; recursion can make divide-and-conquer and tree-like problems clearer but may use more stack memory.","code":"for (int i=1; i<=5; i++) System.out.println(i);","codeExplanation":"The loop performs repeated work without creating a new method call for every iteration.","example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"7.6.1 Naive Recursive Fibonacci","explanation":"Naive Fibonacci calls fib(n-1) and fib(n-2), causing the same subproblems to be recomputed many times.","code":"static long fib(int n) {\n    if (n <= 1) return n;\n    return fib(n-1) + fib(n-2);\n}","codeExplanation":"fib(5) recursively expands into overlapping calls such as fib(3) appearing more than once.","example":null,"exam":"Naive recursive Fibonacci is exponential in time (commonly expressed O(2^n)) and uses O(n) stack depth.","compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"7.6.3 Iterative Fibonacci","explanation":"An iterative Fibonacci solution keeps the previous two values and updates them in a loop, avoiding the recursion tree.","code":"long a=0, b=1;\nfor(int i=0;i<5;i++){\n    System.out.println(a);\n    long next=a+b; a=b; b=next;\n}","codeExplanation":"a and b move forward one Fibonacci position per iteration.","example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"7.6.4 Performance Considerations","explanation":"Choose recursion when it naturally matches the problem and the depth is manageable. Prefer iteration or memoization when naive recursion causes unnecessary repeated work or stack growth.","code":null,"codeExplanation":null,"example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null}]},"8":{"title":"Event-Driven Programming & Event Handling","intro":"Event-driven programs react to events such as clicks, key presses and window actions. The event source generates an event and a registered listener/handler responds.","topics":[{"title":"8.1 Introduction to Event-Driven Programming","explanation":"Control flow is driven by events rather than one fixed sequence of instructions. The program waits for an event and dispatches it to appropriate handlers.","code":null,"codeExplanation":null,"example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"8.2 Event Handling Mechanisms","explanation":"Event handling connects an event source to listener code. A listener implements or registers for a particular event type.","code":"button.addActionListener(e -> System.out.println(\"Clicked\"));","codeExplanation":"The button is the source; the lambda is the listener/handler executed when an ActionEvent occurs.","example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"8.2.1 Types of Events in Java","explanation":"Common GUI events include action events, mouse events, keyboard events, window events and item/selection events.","code":"button.addActionListener(e -> System.out.println(e.getActionCommand()));","codeExplanation":"The action listener responds to the button's action event.","example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"8.3 Listeners and Event Propagation","explanation":"A listener receives an event and performs a response. Registration tells the source which listener should be notified.","code":"button.addActionListener(listener);","codeExplanation":"Without registration, the listener is not connected to the source's event notification mechanism.","example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"8.4 Event Propagation in Java","explanation":"Event dispatching determines how an event moves through the GUI/event system to the relevant handlers. The exact propagation model depends on the toolkit and event type.","code":null,"codeExplanation":null,"example":null,"exam":"Do not assume every event bubbles through parents exactly like a web DOM event.","compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"Event source vs event object","explanation":"The source is the component that generated the event; the event object carries information about what happened and can expose the source.","code":"public void actionPerformed(ActionEvent e) {\n    Object source = e.getSource();\n}","codeExplanation":"getSource() lets the handler identify which registered component generated the event.","example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"Registration","explanation":"Registration establishes the connection between a source and listener.","code":"button.addActionListener(this);","codeExplanation":"The component calls the listener when its action occurs.","example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null}]},"9":{"title":"Exception Handling","intro":"Exceptions represent abnormal conditions that interrupt normal program flow. Java provides structured mechanisms for detecting, propagating and handling these conditions.","topics":[{"title":"9.1 Meaning of Exception Handling in Programming","explanation":"Exception handling allows a program to respond to runtime problems without necessarily crashing immediately. It separates normal logic from error-handling logic.","code":null,"codeExplanation":null,"example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"9.1.1 Java Exception Keywords","explanation":"The key keywords are try, catch, finally, throw and throws. Each has a distinct role in detection, handling or propagation.","code":"try {\n    int x = 10 / 0;\n} catch (ArithmeticException e) {\n    System.out.println(\"Cannot divide by zero\");\n}","codeExplanation":"The risky operation is placed in try; the matching catch handles ArithmeticException.","example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"9.1.2 Types of Exceptions","explanation":"Checked exceptions are checked by the compiler and generally must be caught or declared. Unchecked exceptions are RuntimeException subclasses and are not subject to that compile-time requirement. Errors represent serious JVM/system conditions and are not normally handled like application exceptions.","code":null,"codeExplanation":null,"example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"9.1.3 Exception Hierarchy","explanation":"Throwable is the broad root for exceptions and errors. Exception covers conditions applications may handle; RuntimeException is the common base of unchecked exceptions.","code":"try {\n    throw new IllegalArgumentException(\"bad\");\n} catch (RuntimeException e) {\n    System.out.println(\"runtime\");\n}","codeExplanation":"IllegalArgumentException is a RuntimeException, so the RuntimeException catch matches it.","example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"9.1.4 Common Exception Examples","explanation":"ArithmeticException can result from integer division by zero; NullPointerException can result from dereferencing null; ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException can result from an invalid array index; NumberFormatException can result from invalid numeric parsing.","code":null,"codeExplanation":null,"example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"9.1.5 Exception Handling Methods and Patterns","explanation":"A common pattern is try-catch-finally. Use specific catches before broader catches. Try-with-resources is preferred for AutoCloseable resources because it closes them automatically.","code":"try (Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in)) {\n    int n = sc.nextInt();\n} catch (InputMismatchException e) {\n    System.out.println(\"Invalid number\");\n}","codeExplanation":"The resource declared in try-with-resources is closed automatically when the block finishes.","example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"9.1.6 final vs finally vs finalize","explanation":"final is a keyword for constants, non-overridable methods or non-inheritable classes. finally is a block used with exception handling for cleanup logic. finalize was an old object-finalization mechanism and should not be relied on; modern Java deprecates/removes reliance on it.","code":null,"codeExplanation":null,"example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"9.1.7 throw vs throws","explanation":"throw actually creates/raises a particular exception object at a point in the code. throws appears in a method declaration to state that the method may propagate specified exceptions.","code":"static void check(int age) throws Exception {\n    if (age < 18) throw new Exception(\"Too young\");\n}","codeExplanation":"throws is part of the method signature; throw is the statement that creates/raises the exception here.","example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"9.1.8 Try Block","explanation":"The try block contains code that may throw an exception. A try must be paired with at least one catch or a finally block.","code":"try {\n    risky();\n} finally {\n    System.out.println(\"cleanup\");\n}","codeExplanation":"This form has no catch, but finally is present, so it is syntactically valid if risky() can be called.","example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"Robustness as a Java Design Goal","explanation":"Robust programs anticipate invalid input and exceptional conditions, fail predictably and preserve resources. Exception handling is one part of robustness, not a replacement for validation.","code":null,"codeExplanation":null,"example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"Multiple catch blocks","explanation":"When multiple catches are used, a more specific exception type must come before a broader superclass catch.","code":"try { risky(); }\ncatch (FileNotFoundException e) { }\ncatch (IOException e) { }","codeExplanation":"FileNotFoundException is more specific than IOException, so it appears first.","example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null}]},"10":{"title":"GUI Programming: Swing, JavaFX & AWT","intro":"Java GUI programming uses components, containers, layout managers and event handling to create interactive desktop interfaces. The course compares AWT, Swing and JavaFX.","topics":[{"title":"10.1 Introduction to Java Swing and JavaFX","explanation":"Swing and JavaFX are Java GUI technologies. Swing is built around lightweight components and has been widely used in traditional Java desktop applications; JavaFX provides a newer scene-graph based GUI toolkit.","code":null,"codeExplanation":null,"example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"10.2 Evolution of GUI Frameworks in Java","explanation":"AWT was the early Java GUI toolkit and relies heavily on native peer components. Swing expanded GUI capabilities with lightweight components. JavaFX introduced a more modern scene-graph model and richer UI features.","code":null,"codeExplanation":null,"example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"10.3 Abstract Window Toolkit (AWT)","explanation":"AWT provides foundational GUI components and event APIs and uses native peers for many components.","code":"Frame frame = new Frame(\"AWT\");\nButton b = new Button(\"OK\");\nframe.add(b);\nframe.setSize(300,200);\nframe.setVisible(true);","codeExplanation":"The Frame is a top-level window; Button is a component placed in it.","example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"10.3.1 Swing","explanation":"Swing provides components such as JFrame, JPanel, JButton, JLabel, JTextField and JTable. Swing components are generally lightweight and highly customizable.","code":"JFrame frame = new JFrame(\"Demo\");\nJButton button = new JButton(\"Click\");\nframe.add(button);\nframe.setSize(300,200);\nframe.setVisible(true);","codeExplanation":"JFrame is the window; JButton is a component added to the frame.","example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"10.3.2 JavaFX","explanation":"JavaFX uses a scene graph: a Scene contains a hierarchy of Nodes such as controls and layout panes. JavaFX supports CSS styling and FXML-based UI descriptions.","code":"Button b = new Button(\"Click\");\nStackPane root = new StackPane(b);\nScene scene = new Scene(root, 300, 200);\nstage.setScene(scene);\nstage.show();","codeExplanation":"The stage is the top-level window, scene holds the node hierarchy, and StackPane is a layout container.","example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"10.3.3 Swing vs JavaFX","explanation":"Swing uses components/containers such as JFrame and JPanel; JavaFX uses Stage, Scene and Node-based scene graphs. JavaFX also provides CSS and FXML facilities.","code":null,"codeExplanation":null,"example":null,"exam":"Choose the framework based on the course wording; do not mix Swing class names with JavaFX class names.","compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"10.3.4 Comparison Table: Swing vs JavaFX","explanation":"Swing: mature, traditional, component/container model. JavaFX: newer scene graph, CSS styling, FXML and richer modern UI features. Both support event-driven GUI programming.","code":null,"codeExplanation":null,"example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"10.4 Core Concepts: GUI Application Architecture","explanation":"A GUI application typically has a top-level window, containers/layouts, child components and event handlers. Keeping UI construction separate from application logic improves maintainability.","code":null,"codeExplanation":null,"example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"10.5 Layout Managers and GUI Components","explanation":"Layout managers determine how components are positioned and resized inside containers. Common Swing managers include FlowLayout, BorderLayout, GridLayout, BoxLayout and GridBagLayout.","code":"JPanel p = new JPanel(new FlowLayout());\np.add(new JButton(\"A\"));\np.add(new JButton(\"B\"));","codeExplanation":"FlowLayout places components sequentially and wraps them when needed.","example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"10.5.1 Purpose of Layout Managers","explanation":"They prevent hard-coding every component's coordinates and allow layouts to adapt to window size and platform differences.","code":null,"codeExplanation":null,"example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"10.5.2 Common Swing Layout Managers","explanation":"FlowLayout arranges in a row-like flow; BorderLayout has five regions; GridLayout creates equal-sized grid cells; GridBagLayout provides flexible constraints; BoxLayout arranges along one axis.","code":"JPanel p = new JPanel(new BorderLayout());\np.add(new JButton(\"North\"), BorderLayout.NORTH);\np.add(new JButton(\"Center\"), BorderLayout.CENTER);","codeExplanation":"BorderLayout identifies regions such as NORTH and CENTER.","example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"10.6 GUI Components in Swing","explanation":"Common components include JLabel, JButton, JTextField, JTextArea, JCheckBox, JRadioButton, JComboBox, JList, JTable and JMenuBar.","code":null,"codeExplanation":null,"example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"10.6.1 Menus, Dialogs and Tabbed Interfaces","explanation":"JMenuBar/JMenu/JMenuItem create menus; JOptionPane provides common dialogs; JTabbedPane provides tabbed views.","code":"JOptionPane.showMessageDialog(null, \"Saved\");","codeExplanation":"The call displays a standard Swing message dialog.","example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"10.6.2 Dialogs","explanation":"Dialogs are secondary windows used for messages, input or confirmation. JOptionPane is a common Swing utility for standard dialogs.","code":"int result = JOptionPane.showConfirmDialog(null, \"Continue?\");","codeExplanation":"The result indicates which button the user selected.","example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"10.6.3 Tabbed Interfaces","explanation":"JTabbedPane lets users switch among multiple panels within one container.","code":"JTabbedPane tabs = new JTabbedPane();\ntabs.addTab(\"Home\", new JPanel());\ntabs.addTab(\"Settings\", new JPanel());","codeExplanation":"Two tabs are registered with separate panels.","example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"10.6.4 Complex Event Handling: Mouse, Keyboard, Button","explanation":"MouseListener/MouseAdapter handles mouse events; KeyListener handles key events; ActionListener commonly handles button actions.","code":"button.addActionListener(e -> label.setText(\"Clicked\"));","codeExplanation":"The event handler updates the label when the button action occurs.","example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"10.7 GUI Application Lifecycle","explanation":"A GUI creates components, lays them out, registers handlers and enters the toolkit's event-dispatch mechanism. Swing updates should occur on the Event Dispatch Thread (EDT).","code":null,"codeExplanation":null,"example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null}]},"11":{"title":"Data Structures, Data Visualization & GUI Components","intro":"The photographed course contents place Chapter 11 around data structures in Java, visualization through GUIs, Java GUI frameworks, event handling and data models—not String processing. This chapter has been corrected to match that structure.","topics":[{"title":"11.1 Introduction","explanation":"Chapter 11 connects data structures and GUI presentation. The key idea is separating the data model from the visual components that display or edit it.","code":null,"codeExplanation":null,"example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"11.2 Essentials of Data Structures in Java","explanation":"Java provides arrays, lists, sets, maps, queues and stacks. The correct structure depends on ordering, uniqueness, key-value mapping, access patterns and processing requirements.","code":"List<String> names = new ArrayList<>();\nnames.add(\"Ada\");\nnames.add(\"Grace\");\nSystem.out.println(names.get(0));","codeExplanation":"The list stores ordered values and get(0) returns the first element.","example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"11.3 Benefits of Data Visualization through GUIs","explanation":"A GUI can turn structured data into tables, lists, charts or forms so users can inspect patterns and make decisions more easily. Visualization should clarify rather than obscure the data.","code":null,"codeExplanation":null,"example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"11.4 Introduction to Java GUI Frameworks","explanation":"Swing and JavaFX provide GUI components and event mechanisms. A GUI framework supplies reusable building blocks so developers do not implement every window/control from scratch.","code":null,"codeExplanation":null,"example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"11.4.1 Comparing Swing and JavaFX","explanation":"Swing uses JFrame/JPanel/JButton-style components; JavaFX uses Stage/Scene/Node and supports CSS/FXML. Both can implement event-driven desktop interfaces.","code":null,"codeExplanation":null,"example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"11.4.2 Components and Containers","explanation":"A component is an interactive/display element such as a button or text field. A container holds and organizes components, such as JPanel or a JavaFX Pane.","code":"JPanel panel = new JPanel();\nJButton save = new JButton(\"Save\");\npanel.add(save);","codeExplanation":"panel is the container; save is the component placed inside it.","example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"11.5 Event Handling Basics","explanation":"Event handling connects user actions to application behaviour. A source generates an event and a listener/handler responds.","code":"save.addActionListener(e -> System.out.println(\"Saved\"));","codeExplanation":"The action listener runs when the button action occurs.","example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"11.6 Data Models and GUI Components","explanation":"The model stores or represents application data; GUI components present and manipulate that data. Separating model and view reduces coupling and makes testing easier.","code":"DefaultListModel<String> model = new DefaultListModel<>();\nmodel.addElement(\"Ada\");\nJList<String> list = new JList<>(model);","codeExplanation":"The model stores list data while JList presents it.","example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"11.6.1 Best Practices and Design Patterns","explanation":"Use clear model/view separation, appropriate layout managers, meaningful event handlers and reusable components. Avoid placing all business logic inside GUI event methods.","code":null,"codeExplanation":null,"example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"Data-model consistency","explanation":"When a model changes, the UI should be updated through the toolkit's supported model/notification mechanism. Keeping one source of truth prevents stale or duplicated data.","code":null,"codeExplanation":null,"example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"GUI data structures","explanation":"Lists, tables and combo boxes often expose collection-like data through models. Know whether the component stores the data itself or delegates to a separate model.","code":"DefaultComboBoxModel<String> model = new DefaultComboBoxModel<>();\nmodel.addElement(\"Java\");\nJComboBox<String> box = new JComboBox<>(model);","codeExplanation":"The combo box displays values from its model.","example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null}]},"12":{"title":"Simple I/O, Files & Arrays — Integrated Java Revision","intro":"This chapter is retained as integrated revision material from the earlier hub. It covers common Java input/output, arrays and file-processing patterns; its exact photographed TOC page was not available in the source set used for this correction.","topics":[{"title":"Console output","explanation":"System.out.println prints a value followed by a newline; System.out.print does not add a newline.","code":"System.out.print(\"A\");\nSystem.out.println(\"B\");","codeExplanation":"The output is AB followed by a newline.","example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"Scanner input","explanation":"Scanner reads formatted input from sources such as System.in. nextInt reads an integer; nextLine reads a line of text.","code":"Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);\nint age = sc.nextInt();","codeExplanation":"nextInt consumes the integer token; mixing nextInt and nextLine requires attention to the remaining newline.","example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"Arrays","explanation":"An array stores a fixed number of elements of one declared type and uses zero-based indexing.","code":"int[] a = {10,20,30};\nSystem.out.println(a[1]);","codeExplanation":"The output is 20.","example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"Array traversal","explanation":"Loops are used to inspect or update each array element. Enhanced for is convenient when the index is not needed.","code":"for (int x : a) {\n    System.out.println(x);\n}","codeExplanation":"x receives each element in order.","example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"Files and streams","explanation":"Java I/O APIs provide streams/readers/writers for data movement. Buffered forms reduce repeated low-level operations and can improve efficiency.","code":"try (FileWriter w = new FileWriter(\"out.txt\")) {\n    w.write(\"Hello\");\n}","codeExplanation":"try-with-resources closes the writer automatically.","example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"Arrays utility class","explanation":"java.util.Arrays provides helpers such as sort, binarySearch, equals, fill and toString for arrays.","code":"int[] a={3,1,2};\nArrays.sort(a);\nSystem.out.println(Arrays.toString(a));","codeExplanation":"The output is [1, 2, 3].","example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null}]},"13":{"title":"Control Structures, Methods & Integrated Java — Revision","intro":"This chapter is retained as integrated revision material from the earlier hub. It combines selection, loops, methods, parameter passing, overloading and full-program tracing.","topics":[{"title":"Selection","explanation":"if, if-else and switch choose among execution paths. Conditions are evaluated to decide which block runs.","code":"int x=7;\nif (x > 5) System.out.println(\"A\");\nelse System.out.println(\"B\");","codeExplanation":"The condition is true, so A is printed.","example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"Loops","explanation":"for, while and do-while repeat code. while tests before each iteration; do-while tests after the body and therefore executes at least once.","code":"int i=0;\nwhile(i<3){\n    System.out.println(i);\n    i++;\n}","codeExplanation":"The loop prints 0, 1, 2.","example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"break and continue","explanation":"break exits the nearest loop or switch. continue skips the remainder of the current loop iteration and proceeds to the next iteration.","code":"for(int i=0;i<4;i++){\n    if(i==2) continue;\n    System.out.print(i);\n}","codeExplanation":"2 is skipped, so the output is 013.","example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"Methods","explanation":"A method groups reusable behaviour. A method declaration includes a return type, name, parameter list and body.","code":"static int add(int a,int b){\n    return a+b;\n}","codeExplanation":"add returns the sum of its two parameters.","example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"Pass-by-value","explanation":"Java passes arguments by value. For object references, the value copied is the reference; changing the object's state through that reference can be visible, but reassigning the parameter does not change the caller's reference.","code":"static void change(int x){ x=99; }\nint a=5; change(a);\nSystem.out.println(a);","codeExplanation":"a remains 5 because the primitive value was copied.","example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"Method overloading","explanation":"Methods may share a name when their parameter lists differ. Return type alone is not enough to overload.","code":"static int f(int x){return x;}\nstatic int f(int x,int y){return x+y;}","codeExplanation":"The compiler selects the overload based on the argument list.","example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null},{"title":"Integrated tracing","explanation":"For output questions, trace variables line by line, track loop counters, note method calls/returns and respect evaluation order. Do not guess from the final line alone.","code":"int x=2;\nfor(int i=0;i<3;i++){\n    x += i;\n}\nSystem.out.println(x);","codeExplanation":"x becomes 2, then 2, then 3, then 5; final output is 5.","example":null,"exam":null,"compare":null,"complexity":null}]}};
